course: biology
lesson: 371
title: Street dogs and cats: feeding, safety and never touching a strange animal
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: On the way home from school a scruffy street dog starts trotting behind you. It looks friendly, but you do not know it and it does not know you, so the safe rule is simple: never touch a strange animal.
explain: If a dog comes close, stand still like a tree: arms in, hands together, look away and do not run. Running makes a dog want to chase, just as a ball rolling away does.
explain: Dogs and cats talk with their bodies. A stiff body, raised hair, a growl, a curled lip or a hard stare from a dog, or flat ears, a puffed tail and an arched back on a cat, all say «go away».
explain: In many countries street dogs can carry rabies, a deadly virus in the spit. Any bite or scratch from a strange animal is washed with soap and running water, a grown-up is told, and a doctor is seen the same day.
explain: Many cities and kind people look after street animals: feeding them at fixed places, vaccinating them and having a vet stop them having more babies, so fewer homeless kittens and puppies are born.
fact: Any dog can bite if it is scared, hurt, or guarding its food or puppies, however friendly it looked a moment before.
fact: Running away from a dog can make it chase; standing still with arms in and looking away is safer.
fact: Rabies is a virus carried in the spit of infected animals, and in many countries street dogs are the main source.
fact: Once a person is ill with rabies it almost always kills, but vaccine given quickly after a bite stops it.
fact: A wagging tail does not always mean friendly: a stiff, high wag with a stiff body can mean danger, while a relaxed dog wags its whole wiggly body.
fact: A scared cat flattens its ears, puffs its tail and arches its back; it would rather run, but a cornered cat scratches and bites.
fact: Street animals are best fed by a grown-up at the same place and time, with clean water, and the bowl is put down, never offered from the hand.
fact: Food left lying around brings rats, flies and fights between animals.
fact: Istanbul is famous for its street cats; people put out food, water and little cat houses, and a film about them called Kedi, Turkish for cat, became popular around the world.
fact: Many cities catch street cats and dogs, have a vet do a small operation so they cannot have babies, vaccinate them and let them go back; a clipped ear or ear tag shows it has been done.
fact: For years people reported street dogs in Moscow riding metro trains between stops to reach places with food.
fact: A dog has 42 teeth and a cat has 30, and their long pointed canines make deep narrow holes that trap germs, so even small bites can get infected.
fact: An adult person has at most 32 teeth, and they are blunter than a dog's.
fact: Kittens found alone usually have a mother nearby looking for food, so watch from far away and tell a grown-up rather than picking them up.
exercise: A friendly-looking street dog follows you home. What is the safe rule? | Never touch a strange animal, however friendly it looks | Pat it gently on the head to calm it down before you go in | Give it your sandwich from your hand | Run home fast so it cannot follow
exercise: A strange dog runs up to you. What should you do? | Stand still like a tree, arms in, and look away | Run away as fast as you can and climb something high | Stare it hard in the eyes | Wave your arms and shout at it
exercise: Why can running away from a dog be a bad idea? | Running can make a dog want to chase | Dogs cannot see things that stand still | Running makes a dog lie down and fall asleep | Dogs are always scared of running children
exercise: What is rabies? | A deadly virus carried in the spit of infected animals | A skin rash that dogs and cats get from fleas in the summer | A kind of worm that lives in cats | A cold that dogs give to each other
exercise: A street dog bites your friend, but only a little. What should happen? | Wash it with soap and water, tell a grown-up and see a doctor that day | Wait a week to see if it gets better on its own, and only then tell someone | Put a plaster on and forget about it | Just wash it if it starts to hurt
exercise: Why does a rabies bite need a doctor quickly? | Quick vaccine stops rabies, but once a person is ill it almost always kills | Rabies goes away on its own after a day | A doctor can only help after the person gets ill and has a high fever | Rabies can be cured with any medicine later
exercise: A dog wags its tail stiffly and high, its body stiff and its hair raised. What does it mean? | It may be a warning, so walk away slowly | It is happy and wants to play with you now | It is tired out and wants to lie down and sleep | It is hungry and wants you to give it a stroke
exercise: Which three signs tell you to walk away slowly from a dog? | Raised hair, a growl and a curled lip | A soft body, loose mouth and wiggly wag | Rolling over, yawning and sniffing the ground | Floppy ears, licking lips and lying down
exercise: A stray cat has flat ears, a puffed tail and an arched back. What does that mean? | It is scared and ready to defend itself | It is happy to see you and wants a cuddle | It wants you to pick it up and carry it home | It is just about to curl up and fall asleep
exercise: Why should you never corner a cat in a doorway? | A cornered cat cannot run, so it scratches and bites | Cats get lost in doorways | Cats like being cornered and will purr and rub against your legs | The cat will get stuck in the door
exercise: How should a grown-up feed street cats? | At the same place and time, putting the bowl down with clean water | From the hand, so the cat learns to trust people and comes when called | By throwing food anywhere on the street | Only with sweets and milk at night
exercise: What can food left lying around bring? | Rats, flies and fights between animals | Only happy, healthy cats that grow big and fat | More flowers and grass growing in the yard | Nothing, it just disappears all by itself
exercise: Which city is famous for street cats sleeping in shops and on café chairs? | Istanbul | Reykjavik | Canberra, Australia | Ottawa, Canada
exercise: A street cat has the tip of one ear clipped. What does it show? | A vet has made it unable to have babies and vaccinated it | It lost a fight with a big dog and a piece of its ear was bitten off | It belongs to a rich family | It is a special breed of cat
exercise: What were street dogs in Moscow reported to do? | Ride metro trains between stops to reach food | Drive buses around the city late at night | Sleep all day inside the Kremlin | Swim across the wide river every morning to beg for food
exercise: How many teeth does a dog have? | 42 | 32 | 20 | 52
exercise: You find kittens alone in a box behind a shop. What should you do? | Watch from far away and tell a grown-up | Take them home in your schoolbag to look after | Pick them up and hold them to keep them warm | Chase the mother away so they are all yours
test: «dog's wagging, so it's friendly, i'm gonna pet it» What is right? | A wag is not always friendly, and you never touch a strange dog | Yes, a wagging dog never bites anyone, not even a stranger | Yes, but only with your left hand | Yes, if you pet it very fast
test: In Tom and Jerry, Spike the bulldog turns friendly after Tom gives him a bone. Is a street dog eating a bone safe to pat? | No, dogs may bite to guard their food | Yes, a dog with food is always happy | Yes, food makes every dog calm | Yes, if you pat it while it chews
test: Count them: a dog has 42 teeth and a cat has 30. How many more teeth has the dog? | 12 | 2 | 22 | 72
test: Count them: Granny feeds 5 street cats. How many ears does she see at the bowl? | 10 | 5 | 15 | 20
test: Count them: 3 street dogs follow the bus. How many legs and tails together? | 12 legs and 3 tails | 9 legs and 3 tails | 12 legs and 6 tails | 6 legs and 3 tails
test: «it's only a scratch from a stray cat, chill» What is right? | Wash it, tell a grown-up and get it checked, as scratches can get infected | Scratches from cats never matter, because cats keep their claws very clean | Only bites matter, never scratches | Lick it clean and it will be fine
test: «bro the dog bit me but it's tiny, i'll see if it gets better» Why is waiting a bad idea? | If it carries rabies, vaccine works only if given quickly | Small bites always heal in an hour | Waiting makes the vaccine work better when you finally go | Rabies only comes from big bites
test: What would happen if everyone fed street animals at different spots and times? | Food would lie around and bring rats, flies and fights | The animals would all become pets | The street would stay cleaner | The animals would stop being hungry for ever
test: Why might fewer babies each year be kinder to street cats in the long run? | Fewer kittens are born to go hungry, and the cats already there can live on | It makes the cats grow bigger | Cats with no babies stop eating and so they need much less food | It makes all cats go away at once
test: In a cartoon a dog bites a cartoon boy and he just rubs his leg and laughs. What is right in real life? | A real dog bite makes deep holes that can get infected and needs a doctor | Dog bites never break the skin, so there is nothing to worry about | A laugh cleans the wound | Only cat bites can get infected
test: «shut up, it's my stray, i'll pick up the kittens and take 'em home» What is wiser? | Leave them, the mother may be nearby, and tell a grown-up | Take them, mothers never come back | Shake the box to wake them up | Feed them milk from your hand right away
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person ought to remain motionless, rather than flee, when approached by an unfamiliar dog. | Flight may provoke the animal to give chase | Dogs are incapable of perceiving stationary objects | Running is prohibited in public places | Dogs invariably flee from those who run
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning rabies. | Once symptoms appear it is almost invariably fatal, but prompt vaccination after a bite prevents it | It is a mild illness that resolves without treatment | It is transmitted solely through the air | Vaccination is effective only after the symptoms of the disease have become apparent in the patient
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a clipped ear observed upon a street cat? | It indicates that the animal has been neutered, vaccinated and returned | It indicates injury sustained in combat with a dog or another cat | It denotes ownership by a particular household | It is characteristic of a particular breed
exam: Ascertain the cardinality of the dentition of an adult domestic dog. | Forty-two teeth | Thirty-two teeth | Thirty teeth | Twenty-eight teeth
